NAME
       abort, atexit, exit - ending a program

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdlib.h>

       void abort(void);
       int  atexit(void (*fn)(void));
       void exit(int status);

DESCRIPTION
       exit runs the functions registered with atexit in reverse order,
       flushes stdio and ends the process. abort ends it immediately by
       raising SIGABRT, without running the handlers.

       atexit is the place to put a terminal restore, so a program that
       exits along an unusual path does not leave the console in raw
       mode.

RETURN VALUE
       atexit returns 0 on success. The others do not return.

SEE ALSO
       exit(2), kill(2), termios(3)
